<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Western Satraps</h1><p class="st-meta">35 CE – 415 CE (approx.) · capital Ujjain</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Saka rulers of western India for nearly four centuries, whose king Rudradaman left the first long royal inscription in Sanskrit.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Western Satraps were <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saka">Saka</a> governors who, under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a> or independently, built a state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> in the 1st century. The first line, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshaharata-dynasty">Kshaharatas</a>, reached its height under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahapana">Nahapana</a>. A second line, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kardamaka-dynasty">Kardamakas</a>, was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chastana">Chastana</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a> and became the main dynasty.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They controlled the rich port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharuch">Bharuch</a> and the trade routes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>. Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins, dated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saka-era">Saka era</a> from 78, give an unusually exact chronology. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudradaman-i">Rudradaman I's</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/junagadh-state">Junagadh</a> inscription of 150 boasts of his victories over the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/satavahana-dynasty">Satavahana dynasty</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yaudheya-republic">Yaudheyas</a> and of repairing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudarshana-lake">Sudarshana</a> reservoir, and is written in elegant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, a sign of its spread as a courtly language.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After long rivalry with the Satavahanas, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a> declined in the 4th century. Around 395 to 415 the Gupta emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-ii">Chandragupta II</a> defeated the last ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudrasimha-iii">Rudrasimha III</a>, and annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, opening the western ports to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 35 CE – 415 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:60.40%;width:7.56%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>35 CE – 415 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ujjain</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nahapana, Chastana, Rudradaman I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Hinduism, Jainism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Prakrit, Sanskrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India, Pakistan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Western Kshatrapas, Kshaharatas, Kardamaka dynasty</dd></dl></aside></div>

